#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""check-imports: layering, public-API and one-wrapper-file rules (contracts 2.4 and 2.5).

- Python packages import only their "Internal deps" (contracts 2.2), and each external library only in its one
  wrapper file (contracts 2.5, python layout 1.4). The packages are found by folder, so a rule applies from the
  day its package lands.
- JavaScript packages import only their "Internal deps" (contracts 2.3), the public entry of another package and
  not its files, @rastai/ui/security only inside apps/web, @rastai/client in apps/web only from the three folders
  frontend layout 1 names, and each library only where contracts 2.5 allows it.
- Rust: cargo-deny and check-deps pin each single-owner crate to its owning crate; this check pins the file
  inside the owner (contracts 2.5 "Enforced owners").
- JavaScript specifiers are read from the code, not from the text around it: static imports and re-exports, import(),
  import.meta.glob patterns (a string or an array of strings in its first argument), and JSDoc import types in
  comments. A relative specifier from a package's src/ must stay inside that src/, and every symlink under a scanned
  src/ is a problem: Vite follows symlinks and this check's walk does not. An import.meta.glob call whose options name
  `base` is refused outright: Vite resolves the patterns against base, so it can move them outside src/. Its options
  may not hold a backslash either, since an escape can spell `base` where this check reads plain text.
- .js, .jsx, .mjs, .ts, .tsx, .mts and .cts files are all read the same way, so a TypeScript file in src/generated/ is
  covered (check-no-typescript allows exactly these TypeScript suffixes there).
  Vite detects import.meta.glob calls with a type argument too (import.meta.glob<M>(...)), and so does this check.
Out of scope (a static check can't see these, and they are not a working import in the shipped ESM bundle): a
specifier held in a variable, a ternary or comma expression inside import(), an options object held in a variable,
CommonJS require and .cjs files, and new URL(..., import.meta.url). The real boundary for third-party plugins is the M8
sandboxed iframe.
The tables below restate contracts 2.2, 2.3 and 2.5; tools/checks/tests checks that every package those tables
name has a row here.
"""

